w4.text:
Investigated stacking the best standalone components and inter-op boundaries into `muse_decode_layer`:

1. **Standalone Component Transplant (`GATHB=7` + Attention Tuning)**:
   Transplanted the standalone MoE kernel changes (`GATHB=7` across skinny and fat streams) alongside sliding `decode16` `split8` non-cooperative attention onto the compact T128 QKV/O incumbent baseline. While local paired tests passed reference checks with a negligible ~0.05% aggregate local gain, an official benchmark check on the H200 judge (`56bffe6cfc6a4eae`) passed 13/13 tests and achieved **2.19787x** (95% CI **[2.16491, 2.23084]**), failing the 2.2603x submission gate. The incumbent remains unchanged and was not promoted.

2. **Inter-Op Boundary & Launch Sweep**:
   Evaluated 6 boundary and launch configurations (preload depth, carry width, interleave, finish warps, routing partitions, and serialized vs. PDL execution) across 64 reference-checked cases. Disabling PDL at T16/32 consistently degraded performance by 1.5%–6.6%. Preissuing 1 weight stage instead of 2 provided a minor 0.3%–0.9% latency reduction exclusively on T32 sliding decode, but regressed smaller token counts. Incumbent defaults were preserved.

3. **MoE B4 Column-Parallel Finalization Extension**:
   Extended the B4 column-parallel finalization scratch memory safely from $M \le 32$ up to $M \le 256$ (reserving 128 rows for 2048 routes $\times$ 256 floats). While all 16 reference checks passed, the extension added 1.04–3.60 $\mu$s of overhead across all 8 paired timing benchmarks at T128/T256. The extension was rejected.

Full details, profiles, and diffs are documented in [.ratchet/runs/mvuvm78n-rm78n1/decoder.md](file:///home/nitiz/Projects/Self/Challenges/muse-spark/.ratchet/runs/mvuvm78n-rm78n1/decoder.md) and [.ratchet/runs/mvuvm78n-rm78n1/findings/w4.md](file:///home/nitiz/Projects/Self/Challenges/muse-spark/.ratchet/runs/mvuvm78n-rm78n1/findings/w4.md).

